  "body": "Reported: *\"for certain providers adding keys and configuring them doesn't work, nothing happens or it says application error, opencode go is an example\"* — plus a request to add Azure and check every other provider.\n\nBoth symptoms were real and separate, and the sweep found a third.\n\n## 1. OpenCode Go could not be configured at all\n\n`opencode` and `opencode_go` share `OPENCODE_API_KEY`. `_credential_field_specs` emits **one field per credential**, not per provider, so the second one was deduplicated away. What was left on the card, confirmed in a browser before changing anything:\n\n```json\n{\"provider\":\"opencode_go\",\"fields\":[\"OPENCODE_GO_PROXY\"],\"keyManagers\":0}\n```\n\nOne advanced proxy input. No key field, no rotation, nothing to press. It was the only non-local provider in that state.\n\nThe dedupe is correct — two inputs bound to one variable is two ways to write one value. The key pool is addressed by *variable*, not by provider, so the borrowing card now renders the same add/remove manager, and the owning card keeps the single rotation select. `provider_config_status` gained `credential_owner_id`, `credential_owner_name` and `credential_shared_with` so each card can say where its key actually lives.\n\n## 2. \"Application error\" was the exception class name\n\n`test_provider` returned `type(exc).__name__` and threw the message away:\n\n```\nbefore  {\"ok\":false,\"error_type\":\"ApplicationUnavailableError\"}\nafter   {\"ok\":false,\"error_type\":\"ApplicationUnavailableError\",\n         \"message\":\"GEMINI_API_KEY is not set. Add it to your .env file.\n                    Get a key at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey\"}\n```\n\nThe sentence that says what to do was already being raised. It now goes through `redact_sensitive_error_text` — the same redactor the logs use — and is rendered on the card.\n\n## 3. DeepSeek could not be proxied\n\nThe only remote provider with no `proxy_attr`. Added `DEEPSEEK_PROXY`, plus a contract test so the next provider cannot omit one silently.\n\nCard order also now follows catalog order rather than first-field-seen, which had put OpenCode Go last on the page — nowhere near the account it shares.\n\n## 4. Azure OpenAI\n\nAdded against the **v1 API surface**. `default_base_url` is deliberately `None`: the host carries the customer's own resource name, so any default would be wrong for everyone. The missing-URL error names `AZURE_OPENAI_BASE_URL` instead.\n\n`reasoning_effort` is **not** sent. The model is a customer-chosen deployment name, so nothing in the request reveals whether it is a reasoning model — omitting the field costs thinking on one that is, sending it to one that is not 400s every request. `extra_body` passes through. Same trade-off and same resolution as the existing Alibaba profiles.\n\n## Verification\n\nEvery provider swept through `/admin/api/providers/{id}/test` on the reporter's own server. The chain works; `kimi` returns a genuine `AuthenticationError` — an expired credential, not a code defect.\n\nRe-checked in a browser after the fix: adding a key **from the OpenCode Go card** stores it and flips both cards to Configured.\n\n```json\n{\"storedKeys\":[\"sk-ope…3456\"],\"count\":1,\n \"zenPill\":\"Configured\",\"zenMeta\":\"1 key\",\n \"goPill\":\"Configured\",\"goMeta\":\"1 key\"}\n```\n\nNo `data-key` occurs more than the pre-existing wrapper+input pair, and there are no duplicate element ids — so `changedValues()` is unaffected.\n\nNo Azure key was available, so the wire shape was proved against a fake upstream:\n\n```\nPOST /openai/v1/chat/completions\n  authorization: Bearer azure-secret-key\n  model: my-gpt-5-deployment\n  max_tokens: None\n  max_completion_tokens: 256\n  reasoning_effort: <absent>\n  message roles: ['system', 'user']\nstreamed text: 'Hello from Azure.'\n```\n\nCorrect path, Bearer auth, deployment name as `model`, `max_completion_tokens` rather than `max_tokens` (the o-series and gpt-5 reject the older field), no `api-version` parameter, and text round-tripped end to end.\n\n**Suite: 6 failures on this branch, 6 on unmodified `fork/main` in the same shell, zero new.** The 6 are the known PowerShell installer tests. `ruff format`, `ruff check`, `ty` and the suppression grep all pass.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- **No live request has ever been made against a real Azure resource.** Model listing and streaming are proved against a faithful fake, not the service. First user with a key is the real test. Erred narrow on reasoning for exactly this reason.\n- Azure model listing returns what the endpoint advertises, which may not correspond one-to-one with your deployment names; the deployment name is what `MODEL` needs.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
