    "message": "fix(providers): configurable shared credentials, real test errors, Azure OpenAI\n\nThree defects made providers look broken in the dashboard, plus one new\nprovider.\n\n**OpenCode Go could not be configured at all.** It shares OPENCODE_API_KEY\nwith OpenCode Zen, and the admin manifest emits one field per credential\nrather than per provider, so the deduplication left its card with a single\nadvanced proxy input: no key field, no rotation, no key manager. Pressing\nConfigure did nothing visible. The credential pool is addressed by variable\nrather than by provider, so the fix renders the same add/remove manager on\nthe borrowing card while the owning card keeps the single rotation select --\ntwo controls writing one variable was the thing the dedupe existed to\nprevent. provider_config_status now carries credential_owner_id /\ncredential_owner_name / credential_shared_with so each card can say where its\nkey lives.\n\n**Every failed provider test reported \"ApplicationUnavailableError\".** The\nexception carries the sentence that says what to do (\"GEMINI_API_KEY is not\nset. Add it to your .env file. Get a key at ...\") and test_provider discarded\nit, returning only type(exc).__name__. That is the \"application error\" users\nwere seeing. The message is now returned through redact_sensitive_error_text,\nthe same redactor the logs use, and rendered on the card.\n\n**DeepSeek was the only remote provider that could not be proxied** -- it had\nno proxy_attr. Added DEEPSEEK_PROXY, and a contract test so the next provider\ncannot omit one silently.\n\nCard order now follows provider_status (catalog order) instead of\nfirst-field-seen, which had put OpenCode Go last on the page, nowhere near\nthe account it shares.\n\n**Azure OpenAI** is added against the v1 API surface. default_base_url is\ndeliberately None: the host carries the customer's resource name, so any\nshipped default would be wrong for everyone, and the missing-URL error names\nAZURE_OPENAI_BASE_URL instead. reasoning_effort is not sent -- the model is a\ncustomer-chosen deployment name, so nothing in the request says whether it is\na reasoning model, and sending it to one that is not 400s every request.\nextra_body passes through for users who know which of theirs is which. Same\ntrade-off, and same resolution, as the Alibaba profiles.\n\nVerification:\n\n- Reproduced in a browser against a live server before changing anything:\n  the OpenCode Go card rendered exactly one field, [\"OPENCODE_GO_PROXY\"],\n  with zero key managers.\n- Re-checked in a browser after: added a key from the OpenCode Go card and\n  both cards flipped to Configured, storedKeys count 1 on OPENCODE_API_KEY.\n  No element has more than the pre-existing wrapper+input pair for any\n  data-key, and there are no duplicate element ids.\n- Swept all 35 providers through /admin/api/providers/{id}/test on the\n  reporter's own server. The chain works; kimi returns a genuine\n  AuthenticationError (expired credential, not a code defect).\n- Azure has no key available, so the wire shape was proved against a fake\n  upstream: POST /openai/v1/chat/completions, Authorization: Bearer,\n  model=deployment name, max_completion_tokens=256, max_tokens absent,\n  reasoning_effort absent, no api-version parameter, and text streamed end\n  to end.\n- Full suite: 6 failures on this branch, 6 on unmodified fork/main in the\n  same shell, zero new. The 6 are the known PowerShell installer tests.\n  ruff format, ruff check, ty and the suppression grep all pass.\n\nNot done: no live request has been made against a real Azure resource, so\nmodel listing and streaming are proved against a faithful fake rather than\nthe service. First user with a key is the real test.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
